1
00:00:16,599 --> 00:00:17,866
[engine revving]

2
00:00:22,166 --> 00:00:26,599
[Jerry] This is 
a 1960 Rolls-Royce Silver Cloud II.

3
00:00:27,827 --> 00:00:30,330
It has a 6.2-liter, V8 engine.

4
00:00:30,797 --> 00:00:34,300
The horsepower is listed as "adequate."

5
00:00:36,866 --> 00:00:39,866
It has a very elegant turning radius 
of 42 feet.

6
00:00:51,251 --> 00:00:52,252
[brakes screech]

7
00:00:56,256 --> 00:00:57,257
[brakes screech]

8
00:01:00,927 --> 00:01:02,062
[brakes screech]

9
00:01:06,266 --> 00:01:08,066
[phone ringing]

10
00:01:09,066 --> 00:01:10,233
-[Carl] Hello?
-[Jerry] Carl?

11
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:11,666
-[Carl] Yeah?
-It's Jerry.

12
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:13,700
[Carl] Oh, Jerry! Yes, yes. How are you?

13
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,300
[Jerry] I was in the area. I was wondering
if you would like to grab a coffee.

14
00:01:17,466 --> 00:01:20,300
[Carl] You know something?
I was just about to have breakfast.

15
00:01:20,466 --> 00:01:24,266
-You caught me at a perfect time.
-Fantastic, I'll be by in a few minutes.

16
00:01:28,833 --> 00:01:32,433
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

17
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:35,100
My very special guests today

18
00:01:35,166 --> 00:01:41,566
are comedy legends Carl Reiner
and his pal of 62 years, Mel Brooks.

19
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:48,000
A long time ago, a guy came up
to me and he said,

20
00:01:48,399 --> 00:01:50,500
"Can you give me a buck for a sandwich?"

21
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,100
And I said, "I'll give you a buck
if you promise me one thing."

22
00:01:55,199 --> 00:01:56,233
He says, "What?"

23
00:01:56,399 --> 00:01:57,933
I said, "You go get a drink.

24
00:01:58,418 --> 00:02:00,620
'Cause you look like you can use a drink."

25
00:02:03,766 --> 00:02:05,800
-[Jerry] What do you think of it?
-It is beautiful.

26
00:02:05,866 --> 00:02:07,800
As a car, it's horrible.

27
00:02:08,033 --> 00:02:10,433
-Really?
-Yeah. But it's a nice living room.

28
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:11,733
-Yeah.
-With wheels.

29
00:02:11,799 --> 00:02:12,966
[car roaring]

30
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:14,833
-Was that your car making that noise?
-Yeah.

31
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:16,533
Wow, this is not very elegant.

32
00:02:19,799 --> 00:02:23,766
I bet you nobody knows
that the first four episodes of Seinfeld

33
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:27,000
were shot on the same stage
as The Dick Van Dyke Show.

34
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:28,648
-I remember that.
-Remember that?

35
00:02:28,715 --> 00:02:30,717
-I remember coming...
-I think you came and visited.

36
00:02:30,799 --> 00:02:32,100
The very first show.

37
00:02:45,466 --> 00:02:46,600
[Carl] Have a breakfast menu?

38
00:02:46,666 --> 00:02:49,366
What could they possibly have on the menu
that you're not aware of?

39
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,433
How many breakfasts have you had?

40
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:53,973
-[Carl] No, I want to check the prices.
-Oh.

41
00:02:54,173 --> 00:02:56,442
[Ruthie] My name is Ruthie
and I'll be your server.

42
00:02:56,509 --> 00:02:59,045
-We're gonna be your eaters.
-[Ruthie] Oh, okay!

43
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:05,100
I'm gonna have soft scrambled eggs
and very, very crisp bacon.

44
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:08,266
So crisp that you'll say,
"That's too crisp." That's it.

45
00:03:09,399 --> 00:03:11,299
And I'll have orange juice and coffee.

46
00:03:11,433 --> 00:03:12,700
A cup of coffee.

47
00:03:15,899 --> 00:03:17,833
-Mel comes over every night.
-Every night.

48
00:03:18,066 --> 00:03:19,899
How long is that relationship?

49
00:03:19,966 --> 00:03:21,466
[Carl] From 1950 on.

50
00:03:21,633 --> 00:03:23,899
-So that's...
-[both] 62 years.

51
00:03:23,966 --> 00:03:27,100
First day I met him,
he got up and did a Jewish pirate.

52
00:03:27,366 --> 00:03:28,633
Hysterical.

53
00:03:28,866 --> 00:03:32,366
You know, he said, "I can't afford
to pillage and rape anymore.

54
00:03:32,412 --> 00:03:35,415
Three dollars and 50 cents
for a sailcloth! Who can afford that?"

55
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,299
He went on. And the following day,
I came in and said,

56
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:43,200
"Here's a man who was at the scene
of the Crucifixion 2,000 years ago,"

57
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,366
and I started to interview the 2,000--

58
00:03:46,426 --> 00:03:49,362
Because he had done the pirate,
so you went back even further.

59
00:03:49,429 --> 00:03:51,364
Yeah. I said,
"Here's a man who knows everything."

60
00:03:54,333 --> 00:03:55,966
[Jerry] Okay, so Mel comes over.

61
00:03:56,033 --> 00:03:58,200
About what time at night
would you say he gets there?

62
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:00,899
-Eight o'clock to eat--
-And what do you guys watch?

63
00:04:01,066 --> 00:04:05,633
We like a movie if somebody says,
"Secure the perimeter!"

64
00:04:05,966 --> 00:04:07,899
Anything where they secure the perimeter?

65
00:04:07,947 --> 00:04:09,582
And somebody says, "Get some rest."

66
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:11,000
"Get some rest."

67
00:04:11,333 --> 00:04:13,200
We watch Jeopardy! when we eat.

68
00:04:14,933 --> 00:04:17,700
But wait a minute,
Jeopardy!'s not on at eight o'clock.

69
00:04:17,933 --> 00:04:19,399
-I tape it.
-You...

70
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,133
-I wanna know every detail of this.
-Well, one of the things he does,

71
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,666
and he feels free to do that...
He's watching television.

72
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:31,966
He falls asleep with his mouth open.

73
00:04:32,033 --> 00:04:33,366
Well, that's what old men do.

74
00:04:33,433 --> 00:04:35,900
That's a privilege of old-manness.

75
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,733
That's another thing,
I like to tape baseball games,

76
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:42,833
so I don't have to watch them scratch.

77
00:04:43,416 --> 00:04:44,751
-Ah.
-I can move it forward.

78
00:04:44,817 --> 00:04:45,818
One strike, two strikes.

79
00:04:45,885 --> 00:04:48,621
Yeah, at this age, you wanna...
You don't have time.

80
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:51,500
-Just show me the hits and the catches.
-Compression, compression.

81
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:54,133
Well, that seems like a pretty nice life.

82
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:56,500
-Yeah. It's a good life. Yes.
-I have to say.

83
00:04:56,566 --> 00:04:59,566
The nicest part about it,
and I mean this sincerely,

84
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:03,033
is to have a friend, and that
you guys still live near each other

85
00:05:03,069 --> 00:05:05,371
and get together.
So you never go to his house?

86
00:05:05,438 --> 00:05:07,740
He's an excellent driver.
Well, he's younger.

87
00:05:07,807 --> 00:05:10,376
-He's four, five years younger.
-Right. Yeah, he's a kid.

88
00:05:10,466 --> 00:05:11,433
[chuckles] A kid.

89
00:05:12,666 --> 00:05:15,033
I haven't had bacon in an awful long time.

90
00:05:15,266 --> 00:05:16,533
That's why you're 90.

91
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,433
I get up in the morning,
first thing I read is the obits.

92
00:05:19,599 --> 00:05:21,400
-You don't do that yet. You'll see.
-No.

93
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:22,966
No, I read them. I like them.

94
00:05:23,333 --> 00:05:26,466
I like that they boil
the whole guy's life down to:

95
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:28,633
"Invented the ballpoint pen spring."

96
00:05:29,033 --> 00:05:32,966
If I'm not in the obit, I have breakfast.
That's my whole thing.

97
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,566
We really should tip her before we eat.

98
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:40,166
-We should tip before we eat?
-Yeah.

99
00:05:40,466 --> 00:05:43,966
The timing of the check coming
at the end of the meal makes no sense.

100
00:05:44,433 --> 00:05:47,666
'Cause that's when the food
is the least interesting to you.

101
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:50,733
-When you go in the restaurant...
-That's a great one.

102
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,000
You're so excited.

103
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:56,333
The appetizers and you're anticipating,
"We're gonna have this great night."

104
00:05:56,456 --> 00:05:59,759
Then in the end, there's a cigarette butt
in the mashed potatoes

105
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:01,666
and then they want money for it.

106
00:06:01,933 --> 00:06:03,099
And you're not hungry anymore.

107
00:06:03,166 --> 00:06:05,599
And you can't understand
why you even did this whole thing.

108
00:06:05,666 --> 00:06:08,333
-[Ruthie] Thank you. Please come again.
-[Jerry] Thank you.

109
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:09,400
We haven't left yet.

110
00:06:09,466 --> 00:06:12,466
I had a friend who never picked up
a check without saying,

111
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,700
"They're charging for butter?"

112
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,299
[Jerry] Let me see the room
where you guys watch TV.

113
00:06:22,366 --> 00:06:25,033
[Carl] Oh, here.
Mel brought these yesterday.

114
00:06:25,099 --> 00:06:27,599
[Jerry] Dances with Wolves?
Boy, you're really just catching up.

115
00:06:27,733 --> 00:06:30,433
-[Carl] Anyway, this is where he sits.
-Right.

116
00:06:30,566 --> 00:06:33,333
[Carl] Now, when he eats, he sits here.

117
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:36,233
-[Jerry] The Mel Brooks-Carl Reiner table.
-Right, right.

118
00:06:36,299 --> 00:06:38,500
-You order them on TV?
-Yes, absolutely.

119
00:06:39,065 --> 00:06:40,967
-[Carl] Oh, oh, one more thing.
-[Jerry] Yeah?

120
00:06:41,033 --> 00:06:42,966
-[Carl] Mel is coming over tonight.
-[Jerry] Right.

121
00:06:43,033 --> 00:06:45,333
-[Carl] Would you like to come?
-[Jerry] That would be a thrill.

122
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:46,733
I would love to do that.

123
00:06:52,333 --> 00:06:55,333
-[Carl] Nate 'n Al's. What do you want?
-[Jerry] You like hot tongue sandwich?

124
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:56,733
-[Carl] Yes.
-[Jerry] What does Mel like?

125
00:06:56,783 --> 00:06:58,818
-[Carl] Chicken in a pot.
-[Jerry] Chicken in a pot.

126
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:01,000
-[Jerry] All right. Fantastic.
-[Carl] Okay, so 8:00.

127
00:07:01,066 --> 00:07:02,366
-[Carl] See you.
-[Jerry] All right.

128
00:07:03,222 --> 00:07:04,424
[man] Hey, Jerry!

129
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:22,400
[Jerry humming]

130
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:24,933
-Who's that?
-It's probably Seinfeld.

131
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,099
[Jerry] Hello?

132
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,866
-[Carl] Did you bring food?
-I did.

133
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:30,633
Hi, Mel.

134
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:32,733
Well, this looks like potato salad.

135
00:07:33,666 --> 00:07:36,766
I want sandwiches, I want chicken.
Did you bring soup too?

136
00:07:37,466 --> 00:07:38,799
-Look at Carl working.
-Really.

137
00:07:40,159 --> 00:07:42,862
-Guy gets hit by a car, little Jewish man.
-[Jerry] Yeah. Mm-hmm.

138
00:07:42,929 --> 00:07:44,530
Friend says,
"Get a pillow. Do something.

139
00:07:44,866 --> 00:07:47,533
Put it under his head."
He says, "All right, are you comfortable?"

140
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:50,233
And the guy says, "I make a living."

141
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:51,471
[Jerry laughs]

142
00:07:51,566 --> 00:07:53,599
I've never heard anyone
do the punchline like that.

143
00:07:53,833 --> 00:07:55,566
Are you turkey or roast beef?

144
00:07:55,799 --> 00:07:57,666
This thing just keeps on going.

145
00:07:57,766 --> 00:07:59,400
[Mel] My doctor says don't eat salt.

146
00:07:59,466 --> 00:08:01,066
-So I don't eat salt.
-Do you miss it?

147
00:08:01,133 --> 00:08:02,633
No one has ever asked me that.

148
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,700
We're all, you know, comic...

149
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:08,066
-Luminaries.
-Yeah, luminaries, yeah.

150
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:10,400
-[Jerry] What do you think "R.B." is?
-[Mel] Roast beef.

151
00:08:10,733 --> 00:08:13,200
-You're ready for Jeopardy!, I can see.
-Oh, yeah.

152
00:08:14,066 --> 00:08:16,033
[Mel] What are you eating,
corned beef or pastrami?

153
00:08:16,099 --> 00:08:17,866
[Jerry] The roast beef. Well, cheers.

154
00:08:17,933 --> 00:08:19,599
Thank you for doing this, by the way.

155
00:08:20,066 --> 00:08:21,533
Do you have any idea what you're doing?

156
00:08:22,433 --> 00:08:23,633
No. That's good.

157
00:08:27,466 --> 00:08:29,566
Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.
I met a girl.

158
00:08:29,733 --> 00:08:31,299
She was incredibly thin.

159
00:08:31,366 --> 00:08:34,699
I took her to a restaurant.
This girl was so thin, the maître d' said,

160
00:08:34,766 --> 00:08:36,366
"Check your umbrella."
I'm not making this up.

161
00:08:36,466 --> 00:08:38,333
But you don't do that.

162
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,366
-You do characters.
-No.

163
00:08:40,733 --> 00:08:42,699
-No, well, you do--
-Carl, tell him what I do.

164
00:08:44,733 --> 00:08:48,733
You know, Mel,
you have never seen him work in person.

165
00:08:49,333 --> 00:08:51,766
-I've always seen--
-What are you bothering him for?

166
00:08:51,833 --> 00:08:53,266
No, because I will.

167
00:08:53,533 --> 00:08:54,533
No, you won't.

168
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:56,800
Now I will. But do you ever work anymore?

169
00:08:56,966 --> 00:08:58,566
You said you're going to Caesars.

170
00:08:58,633 --> 00:09:00,133
Yeah, but you're not going to Caesars.

171
00:09:00,199 --> 00:09:01,533
No, no, I'm not gonna go.

172
00:09:02,833 --> 00:09:04,633
You know who I admire lately?

173
00:09:04,833 --> 00:09:06,500
And not because I think he's funny.

174
00:09:06,566 --> 00:09:09,600
I admire his commercial aptitude.

175
00:09:09,866 --> 00:09:11,766
-[Carl] Who is that?
-Louis C.K.

176
00:09:11,817 --> 00:09:13,519
[Jerry] His business acumen.
Is that what you mean?

177
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:14,433
Yes!

178
00:09:14,487 --> 00:09:17,156
[Jerry] One thing for sure, Mel,
is this is getting cut out.

179
00:09:17,366 --> 00:09:19,933
I'll be damned if I'm gonna sit here
hyping Louis C.K.

180
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:20,966
I don't blame you.

181
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:25,500
Get Smart. That was a show
that changed my life.

182
00:09:25,566 --> 00:09:26,800
He just played it here last night.

183
00:09:26,933 --> 00:09:29,366
-I got Buck Henry to come over.
-Yeah.

184
00:09:29,433 --> 00:09:33,533
We watched the pilot, I said,
"Buck, just for my box set, do me a favor.

185
00:09:34,466 --> 00:09:37,300
Let's watch the pilot again,
see what we can remember."

186
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:40,112
He said,
"I came up with the Cone of Silence."

187
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:41,300
Really?

188
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:43,433
"You came up with the Shoe Phone."

189
00:09:43,566 --> 00:09:46,166
Oh, God, you're breaking my heart.
I love that show.

190
00:09:50,033 --> 00:09:51,833
Every day, I went to Chock full o'Nuts.

191
00:09:52,133 --> 00:09:55,466
And a guy started bothering me,
started pulling at my coat.

192
00:09:55,528 --> 00:09:56,362
[chuckles]

193
00:09:56,429 --> 00:09:57,630
Norman Steinberg.

194
00:09:57,964 --> 00:09:59,732
-Jewish fella?
-Wonderful writer.

195
00:10:00,633 --> 00:10:04,133
And he said, "I'm a lawyer.
I don't wanna be a lawyer.

196
00:10:04,203 --> 00:10:06,238
-[Jerry chuckles]
-I wanna be a comedy writer."

197
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:09,833
So I said, "Look,
I have a show called Get Smart.

198
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:12,966
See the show and write
a Get Smart episode."

199
00:10:13,266 --> 00:10:15,233
Agent 99 and 86 and...

200
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:16,766
So he did.

201
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:19,033
He wrote a couple of Get Smarts.

202
00:10:19,585 --> 00:10:21,887
I don't know if they used that show,
but they used him.

203
00:10:21,966 --> 00:10:23,566
It was like The Godfather.

204
00:10:23,833 --> 00:10:25,766
I said, "You owe me.

205
00:10:25,825 --> 00:10:26,792
[laughs]

206
00:10:27,199 --> 00:10:30,199
I want you to be one of the writers
on Blazing Saddles."

207
00:10:30,333 --> 00:10:32,400
That's the most amazing story
I've ever heard.

208
00:10:32,466 --> 00:10:34,533
-That story doesn't usually go like that.
-[Carl and Mel] No.

209
00:10:36,333 --> 00:10:38,800
To me, when you take a big chance

210
00:10:38,866 --> 00:10:43,900
is when you bring
a posse of cowboys onto a soundstage

211
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:47,433
where they're doing a gay
Busby Berkeley dance number.

212
00:10:47,766 --> 00:10:50,766
I saw that a few weeks ago and I thought,

213
00:10:51,366 --> 00:10:53,199
-"The balls on this guy."
-[Carl] Yeah.

214
00:10:53,733 --> 00:10:55,733
Were you absolutely sure
that this was gonna work?

215
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:56,766
No.

216
00:10:56,833 --> 00:10:59,433
Whenever I lecture or teach
or do anything,

217
00:10:59,566 --> 00:11:01,133
I say, "It's gotta please you.

218
00:11:01,193 --> 00:11:02,661
If you don't laugh,
they're not gonna laugh."

219
00:11:02,900 --> 00:11:05,033
-Right.
-I knew it tickled me.

220
00:11:05,699 --> 00:11:09,133
I even wrote a line
where one of the gay dancers says

221
00:11:09,833 --> 00:11:11,933
to a rough cowboy after he fights...

222
00:11:11,971 --> 00:11:14,440
He fights with him, and he cries,
and the cowboy pets him,

223
00:11:14,507 --> 00:11:17,276
and the cowboy says,
"I'm parked just behind the commissary."

224
00:11:18,511 --> 00:11:21,514
Billy Wilder said,
"The reason I'm having lunch with you now

225
00:11:21,699 --> 00:11:25,233
is because you had 100 cowboys

226
00:11:26,533 --> 00:11:30,633
break through a set at Warner Brothers
with gay dancers."

227
00:11:31,466 --> 00:11:35,966
He said, "I've been around,
I've never seen anything like that."

228
00:11:36,333 --> 00:11:38,733
If Billy Wilder likes it,
I must be good, because...

229
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:40,533
-I mean, he was my god.
-You're good.

230
00:11:41,633 --> 00:11:44,300
Interestingly, both of these aspects of...

231
00:11:44,366 --> 00:11:48,433
Both of you guys did things
that are inside-show-business things.

232
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:50,199
A joke about the commissary,

233
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:52,666
you would think,
"Who's gonna know what a commissary is?"

234
00:11:52,766 --> 00:11:53,766
[Mel] Yeah, I didn't care.

235
00:11:53,833 --> 00:11:55,466
"Who's gonna know
what sitcom writers are?"

236
00:11:58,633 --> 00:12:03,100
I needed a black guy to justify
our inordinate use of the N-word.

237
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,000
And Richie Pryor was a good friend
of mine, we used to hang out together.

238
00:12:06,199 --> 00:12:07,333
So he joined us.

239
00:12:07,866 --> 00:12:12,233
So I had Andrew Bergman,
Richie Pryor, Norman, and Alan Uger.

240
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:15,333
-He had a partner, a dentist...
-A lawyer and a dentist.

241
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:17,766
He never gave up being a dentist,
this guy, Alan Uger.

242
00:12:17,833 --> 00:12:20,199
But he wrote comedy on the side.
He was very good.

243
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:21,333
You're kidding.

244
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:23,600
The five of us wrote Blazing Saddles.

245
00:12:23,966 --> 00:12:27,433
We wrote a lot of it in New York
and then I took Norman with me.

246
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:32,300
-When I go to a comedy club...
-Why would you go to a comedy club?

247
00:12:32,518 --> 00:12:35,421
-Sometimes I'm interested in attitudes.
-[Jerry] Right.

248
00:12:35,533 --> 00:12:37,933
-[Mel] I smell comedy, I really do.
-Yeah.

249
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:41,400
I knew very early at the Village Vanguard
when I saw Richie Pryor.

250
00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:43,863
-[Jerry] Right. Right.
-Became his pal. "This guy got it."

251
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:46,866
Chris Rock, I saw him.
Right away, I said, "He's funny."

252
00:12:46,932 --> 00:12:49,735
Chris Rock, that was '96. This is 2012.

253
00:12:50,233 --> 00:12:52,800
That's a long time
between guys breaking out.

254
00:12:52,866 --> 00:12:54,966
This is 2012?

255
00:12:55,366 --> 00:12:58,033
Holy... I have so many appoint--

256
00:12:58,266 --> 00:13:02,699
I'm sorry, this is terrible to do, but I--
2012? Holy shit.

257
00:13:04,766 --> 00:13:06,266
-So I had this idea...
-Right.

258
00:13:06,333 --> 00:13:09,266
...about this movie
about Bialystock and Bloom,

259
00:13:09,333 --> 00:13:10,800
about the ego and the id.

260
00:13:10,866 --> 00:13:12,033
I mean, I was driven.

261
00:13:12,166 --> 00:13:16,233
If he had spent his time with this,
he wouldn't have never done Producers.

262
00:13:16,566 --> 00:13:19,833
I knew that.
I kind of knew if I got stuck in a sitcom,

263
00:13:20,633 --> 00:13:22,366
it's a swamp. It's quicksand.

264
00:13:22,501 --> 00:13:23,903
-You're telling me.
-[chuckles]

265
00:13:27,099 --> 00:13:30,599
When The Producers came out,
the play, is that...

266
00:13:30,666 --> 00:13:35,866
You thought that there was
a profound revenge for Hitler's crimes

267
00:13:36,066 --> 00:13:37,199
in making fun of him?

268
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:42,033
Kenny Mars came in and read it,
and I was stunned.

269
00:13:42,233 --> 00:13:45,599
[speaking in German]

270
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:47,166
I mean, he was so German.

271
00:13:47,233 --> 00:13:51,466
But the very fact that he did
the definitive work against Hitler...

272
00:13:51,530 --> 00:13:53,432
-[Jerry] Yes. Right.
-...in the world,

273
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:57,199
and that the musical played in Germany

274
00:13:57,533 --> 00:13:59,500
-and was the biggest hit, lately.
-Really?

275
00:13:59,566 --> 00:14:01,766
Yes. I won number one play.

276
00:14:02,099 --> 00:14:06,400
In Berlin that season, I won
the Ernst Lubitsch Award for theater.

277
00:14:06,533 --> 00:14:07,733
The very fact that you--

278
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:10,533
I don't know whether they really liked it
or they were apologizing.

279
00:14:10,599 --> 00:14:11,933
-[Carl] No.
-[Mel] I'm not sure.

280
00:14:12,766 --> 00:14:14,300
♪ Springtime ♪

281
00:14:14,733 --> 00:14:20,166
Hank Mancini had made
an instrumental version

282
00:14:20,266 --> 00:14:21,699
of "Springtime for Hitler."

283
00:14:21,794 --> 00:14:23,596
[Mancini's
"Springtime for Hitler" playing]

284
00:14:23,666 --> 00:14:26,666
With a... [humming] ...a little Latin.

285
00:14:27,133 --> 00:14:29,199
[continues humming]

286
00:14:31,503 --> 00:14:33,505
-[Carl laughs]
-[Mel mimics trumpet]

287
00:14:33,699 --> 00:14:35,033
And it's playing,

288
00:14:35,099 --> 00:14:36,900
and I'm with a bunch of people
in an elevator,

289
00:14:36,966 --> 00:14:38,666
a nice lady, and they're swaying.

290
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:42,766
And I said,
"If they only knew the lyrics," you know?

291
00:14:45,266 --> 00:14:46,800
It's challenging and it's fun.

292
00:14:46,933 --> 00:14:49,800
That's why you jumped
into little clubs to say,

293
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,933
with brand-new material
that is bound to fail.

294
00:14:52,992 --> 00:14:54,326
-[Jerry] Right.
-"How can I...

295
00:14:54,393 --> 00:14:56,161
If I can get two laughs tonight."

296
00:14:56,233 --> 00:15:00,800
Right. I could talk about comedy a lot,
but I think it's like talking about sex.

297
00:15:00,866 --> 00:15:04,166
You can do any fancy dive you want,
it's just a foot of water.

298
00:15:04,270 --> 00:15:06,939
[chuckles] That's funny.
Did you ever say this before?

299
00:15:07,033 --> 00:15:08,066
-No.
-[Mel] That's good.

300
00:15:10,233 --> 00:15:13,266
[Jerry] How is this possible?
That you have this mug?

301
00:15:13,466 --> 00:15:14,699
"Old Jews telling jokes."

302
00:15:14,766 --> 00:15:16,566
I said, "I'm an old Jew,
I'm gonna tell a joke."

303
00:15:16,633 --> 00:15:18,433
This is a comedy class.

304
00:15:18,599 --> 00:15:23,000
Morty sees Moshe on the street and says,

305
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:25,333
"Moshe, I heard about the fire!"

306
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:26,733
And he said, "Shh!...

307
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:27,866
[whispers] Thursday."

308
00:15:27,926 --> 00:15:29,128
[Jerry and Carl laugh]

309
00:15:29,199 --> 00:15:32,199
See, you get it?
See, look at all the acting I did.

310
00:15:32,298 --> 00:15:34,700
-For a lousy joke!
-I saw it. To me, you're royalty.

311
00:15:34,767 --> 00:15:36,402
[Mel] Oh... [clicks tongue]

312
00:15:36,935 --> 00:15:38,771
I'm just a prince, I'm not a king.

313
00:15:38,866 --> 00:15:41,766
I went to see Alan King at the Meadowland,

314
00:15:41,833 --> 00:15:45,800
and I saw Alan King do two hours
of the most brilliant stuff

315
00:15:45,866 --> 00:15:47,666
on his family, his brothers.

316
00:15:47,766 --> 00:15:51,466
I met you at an Alan King show
at the Westbury Music Fair...

317
00:15:51,517 --> 00:15:54,920
That's it! That's the time.
I only saw him one time in my whole life.

318
00:15:55,354 --> 00:15:57,356
-That's... that's it.
-I was eight years old.

319
00:15:57,466 --> 00:15:59,133
-[Mel] You're kidding. Is this true?
-Yes.

320
00:15:59,358 --> 00:16:00,759
And I snuck backstage.

321
00:16:00,826 --> 00:16:03,128
There was a little dressing room,
I walked in it.

322
00:16:03,699 --> 00:16:06,300
You were sitting there.
Alan King was there.

323
00:16:06,865 --> 00:16:08,934
I got his autograph and your autograph.
I still have it.

324
00:16:09,034 --> 00:16:10,736
-Wow. Is this true?
-Yeah!

325
00:16:13,933 --> 00:16:16,466
♪ I've been away from you a long time ♪

326
00:16:16,533 --> 00:16:17,866
♪ I never thought ♪

327
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,766
♪ Somehow I feel your love is real
Near you ♪

328
00:16:20,866 --> 00:16:23,966
This was a surprisingly
enjoyable experience.

329
00:16:24,033 --> 00:16:25,300
-Really?
-Yes. All right.

330
00:16:25,366 --> 00:16:26,233
Thanks again, Mel.

331
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:28,733
-[Mel] All right. I love you.
-Love you, too. Great to see you.

332
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:31,366
-All right, ladies and germs. Okay.
-[Jerry] All right.

333
00:16:31,433 --> 00:16:33,533
Have a good night. That is a gorgeous car.

334
00:16:33,599 --> 00:16:34,800
I'm glad you like it.

335
00:16:34,866 --> 00:16:36,800
-What year was it?
-[Jerry] 1970.

336
00:16:36,866 --> 00:16:39,933
I was five. Good night, everybody!
Wonderful working with you.

337
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,166
-Stop that.
-[Carl] Jerry.

338
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:43,733
What's the difference
between a Jew and a Frenchman?

339
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:46,666
A Frenchman leaves
without saying good-bye.

340
00:16:46,705 --> 00:16:49,007
-[Jerry] Yeah.
-[Carl and Mel] And a Jew says good-bye

341
00:16:49,074 --> 00:16:50,776
-and never leaves.
-[Jerry] Ha!

342
00:16:51,133 --> 00:16:52,500
That was a great joke.

343
00:17:01,286 --> 00:17:04,957
[Jeopardy theme song playing]

